    {
      "card_ref": "EV-PHY-11-MIP-07-011",
      "independent_solution": [
        "(b) is the definition of average velocity and (e) of average acceleration, so both hold for any motion.",
        "(a) needs v linear in t, (c) and (d) need a constant a; e.g. for a(t) = kt, v = v0 + kt^2/2 != v0 + a(t)t. So (a), (c), (d) are not true in general."
      ],
      "independent_answer": "(b) and (e)",
      "official_answer": "(b) and (e)",
      "agrees": true
    },
    {
      "card_ref": "EV-PHY-11-MIP-07-013",
      "independent_solution": [
        "Read options from the pinned pages 3-4 (rendered): (a) v_av = (1/2)[v(t1)+v(t2)], (b) v_av = [r(t2)-r(t1)]/(t2-t1), (c) r = (1/2)(v(t2)-v(t1))(t2-t1), (d) a_av = [v(t2)-v(t1)]/(t2-t1).",
        "(b) and (d) are definitions, always correct. (a) holds only for constant acceleration; (c) is wrong in general (even for constant a the displacement is (1/2)(v1+v2)(t2-t1), and a body at constant velocity would give r = 0). Incorrect ones: (a) and (c)."
      ],
      "independent_answer": "(a), (c)",
      "official_answer": "(a), (c)",
      "agrees": true
    },
    {
      "card_ref": "EV-PHY-11-MIP-07-015",
      "independent_solution": [
        "Continuation of Exemplar 4.13 holding options (c) and (d).",
        "(c) r = (1/2)(v(t2)-v(t1))(t2-t1) is incorrect in general (uniform motion gives v(t2) = v(t1) but non-zero displacement); (d) is the definition of average acceleration and is correct."
